> On Sat, Aug 7, 2010 at 4:34 PM, Luke Bergen <lukeabergen@gmail.com> wrote=
:
> > So I saw this old school juke box and thought to myself "that's cool".
> >
> > What would be the closest lojban for this?=A0 I'm looking for a word th=
at is
> > generic and means "good" with a connotation of being stylistic, popular=
,
> > attractive (physically, conceptually, or otherwise), etc...
> >
> > In other words, if someone saw a really sexy sports car driving down th=
e
> > road in jbogu'e what might he say?
>=20
> "Cool" is a cool word, huh?=A0 We can be sure there's no gismu even
> vaguely similar to "cool" because it would have been considered the
> worst malglico in the world. 8)
>=20
> If you want exactly the connotations of "cool" what you want of course
> is a fu'ivla.=A0

Why "of course"?=A0 I realize that lojbanistan is dominated by English spea=
kers,=20
but it's not a language for English speakers.=A0 fui'vla should be based on=
=20
either words that are recognizable in many or most languages,=20
or on international nomneclature systems like the linnaean.=A0 In how=20
many languages does 'cool' mean 'cool'?

totus

=A0You can even get all the various senses of "cool": za'e
> {xamgrkulu} or za'e {zabnrkulu} could mean like "wow, look at that,
> that's cool" while za'e {jikcrkulu} could mean more like "no worries,
> we're cool man" and maybe za'e {cninrkulu} could mean being the latest
> hippest thing, etc.=A0 Then you can say:
>=20
> {.i ko na xanka .i mi'o jikcrkulu .i do zabnrkulu pe'i .i xu do djica
> lo nu do mi kansa lo cninrkulu} =3D "No worries, man.=A0 We're cool.=A0 I
> think you're cool.=A0 Want to go do what's cool?"
>=20
> We should also be thinking about some lujvo on -xau (xamgu) and -za'a
> (zabna) and -nei (nelci) and such for the subtleties of grooving upon
> things, but I don't think we should try to reproduce any natlang
> meanings there.=A0 Instead, we should explore new distinctly Lojbanic
> flavors of goodness. 8)
